# Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the F1 Pitwall Analytics project.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics@^2.0.1` package
- Updated package.json with the new dependency
- Updated package-lock.json with resolved dependencies

### 2. Code Configuration
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next';`
- Inserted `<Analytics />` component at the end of the body element, after the `</AuthProvider>` closing tag
- Placement ensures Analytics tracks all routes and user interactions while maintaining existing functionality

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Analytics quickstart documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), I:
1. Retrieved the latest installation instructions using WebFetch
2. Identified this as a Next.js 14 App Router project
3. Applied the framework-specific configuration for Next.js App Router
4. Placed the Analytics component in the root layout to track all pages

## Verification

All quality checks passed successfully:
- ✅ ESLint: No warnings or errors
- ✅ Build: Compiled successfully (14 routes generated)
- ✅ Tests: All 308 tests passed across 39 test files
- ✅ Lock files: package-lock.json properly updated

## Next Steps

To complete the setup:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Project Settings → Analytics)
3. Analytics data will be available in the dashboard after users visit the site

## Notes

- The Analytics component is placed outside the AuthProvider to ensure it tracks all visitors, not just authenticated users
- The component uses the `@vercel/analytics/next` import which provides seamless Next.js integration with automatic route tracking
- No environment variables or additional configuration required for basic functionality
